-- Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab) gap-filling MCQs PHY-1501 to PHY-1645 (145 questions): topics, worked
-- examples, formulas and textbook boxes that PHY-0001 to PHY-0446 did not test, or tested only once. Original questions
-- in the bank's template with the source page in the textbook. questions.chapter is the MDCAT chapter (the AKU chapter
-- for questions off the MDCAT syllabus); FSc Part 2 lists every question under its textbook chapter. Not in the free
-- set. INSERT OR IGNORE keeps the file safe to run twice; on a fresh local database nothing is inserted.

-- It also re-checks the mapping of the existing Physics 12 questions against the PMDC MDCAT 2025 curriculum and the
-- NUMS MDCAT 2026 syllabus, and applies the same rules to the new ones:
--   * Chapter 13 (kinetic theory, stars): both syllabi's thermodynamics is only the first law and specific heats, so
--     these questions leave the MDCAT and NUMS banks (AKU keeps them).
--   * Chapter 17: rectification moves to Electronics and mutual induction to Electromagnetic induction (both are named
--     in the PMDC and NUMS syllabi). Impedance, RC/RL/RLC circuits, AC power, self-induction and energy in an inductor
--     are on neither, so those questions leave the MDCAT bank. NUMS has no AC unit, so the rest of chapter 17 stays
--     MDCAT only.
--   * Chapter 18: Compton effect, pair production and annihilation, de Broglie waves, Davisson-Germer, wave-particle
--     duality, the electron microscope and the uncertainty principle are on neither syllabus.
--   * Chapter 19: particle physics (annihilation reactions, the electroweak bosons), mass defect and binding energy,
--     fission, chain reactions, reactors, nuclear waste and fusion are on neither syllabus; the nucleus, radioactive
--     decay, half-life, activity, tracers and uses of radiation stay.
--   * NUMS names only some subtopics in its units, so damping, resonance, forced oscillations and energy in SHM
--     (Waves) and combinations of capacitors and the capacitance of a sphere (Electrostatics) stay out of the NUMS bank.

-- Inserted in batches of 20 to stay under D1's statement size limit.

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-1501', 'Thermodynamics', 'Root-mean-square speed', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 5 (§13.3)', 'Why does kinetic theory use the mean of the squared velocities of gas molecules instead of their simple average velocity?', '["The simple average velocity is zero, because velocities in opposite directions cancel.", "Squaring makes the velocities smaller and easier to measure.", "Molecules only move in one direction, so averaging is not needed.", "The simple average velocity is always larger than the speed of light."]', 0, 'A molecule moving with +v before hitting a wall moves with −v after it, and molecules move equally in all directions, so the plain average of the velocities is zero. Squaring first removes the signs, and the square root of the mean square gives the rms speed.', '{"1": "Squaring a speed greater than 1 m s⁻¹ makes it larger, and the reason is about signs, not size.", "2": "Molecules move randomly in all directions; that is exactly why the plain average cancels to zero.", "3": "Molecular speeds are a few hundred metres per second, far below the speed of light."}'),
  ('PHY-1502', 'Thermodynamics', 'Pressure of a gas', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 7 (§13.4)', 'If n is the number of molecules per unit volume and <K.E.> is their average translational kinetic energy, the pressure of an ideal gas is', '["P = ⅓n<K.E.>", "P = (3/2)n<K.E.>", "P = ⅔n<K.E.>", "P = n<K.E.>"]', 2, 'P = ⅓(N/V)m<v²> = ⅔(N/V)(½m<v²>) = ⅔n<K.E.>. So pressure is two-thirds of the translational kinetic energy per unit volume.', '{"0": "⅓ goes with m<v²>, not with ½m<v²>; rewriting in terms of kinetic energy doubles it to ⅔.", "1": "3/2 is the factor in <K.E.> = (3/2)k_BT, not in the pressure formula.", "3": "This leaves out the factor ⅔ that comes from averaging over three directions."}'),
  ('PHY-1503', 'Thermodynamics', 'Pressure of a gas', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 3 (§13.3)', 'A gas is sealed in a rigid container and heated. According to kinetic theory, why does its pressure rise?', '["The molecules become larger and take up more space.", "The number of molecules in the container increases.", "The molecules start attracting each other strongly.", "The molecules move faster, so they hit the walls more often and with more momentum."]', 3, 'Average kinetic energy is proportional to absolute temperature, so heating makes the molecules move faster. Faster molecules strike the walls more frequently and with a larger change in momentum, so the force per unit area rises.', '{"0": "Kinetic theory treats the size of the molecules as fixed and negligible.", "1": "The container is sealed, so the number of molecules stays the same.", "2": "Kinetic theory assumes no forces between molecules except during collisions."}'),
  ('PHY-1504', 'Thermodynamics', 'Daily-life examples', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 8 (§13.4 (Daily life examples))', 'When a popcorn kernel is heated it bursts open. According to the book, this happens because', '["the starch inside melts and expands like a liquid", "moisture trapped inside turns to steam and the pressure inside the kernel rises", "air outside the kernel is pushed in by heating", "the kernel loses all its molecules by evaporation"]', 1, 'Heating turns the trapped moisture into steam. The steam molecules move fast and raise the pressure inside the kernel until it ruptures the cover; the released starch sets solid on cooling.', '{"0": "The starch is released after the cover breaks; it is the steam pressure that breaks it.", "2": "Heating raises the pressure inside the kernel, not outside it.", "3": "The kernel bursts because of a pressure build-up, not a loss of molecules."}'),
  ('PHY-1505', 'Thermodynamics', 'Brownian motion', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 2 (§13.1)', 'In the discussion of Brownian motion, viscosity of a fluid is described as', '["the internal friction of the fluid, its resistance to flow", "the density of the fluid", "the average speed of the fluid molecules", "the temperature at which the fluid boils"]', 0, 'Viscosity measures the internal friction in a fluid, its resistance to flow. A less viscous fluid gives faster Brownian movement.', '{"1": "Density is mass per unit volume, a different property.", "2": "Molecular speed depends on temperature, not on what viscosity measures.", "3": "The boiling point is unrelated to viscosity."}'),
  ('PHY-1506', 'Thermodynamics', 'Kinetic theory and gas laws', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 3 (§13.3)', 'How many moles of gas are there in 3.01 × 10²³ molecules? (N_A = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹)', '["2.0 mol", "1.81 × 10⁴⁷ mol", "0.25 mol", "0.50 mol"]', 3, 'The number of moles is n = N/N_A = 3.01 × 10²³ ÷ 6.02 × 10²³ = 0.50 mol.', '{"0": "2.0 mol is N_A ÷ N, the inverted ratio.", "1": "This multiplies N by N_A instead of dividing.", "2": "0.25 mol would need only 1.505 × 10²³ molecules."}'),
  ('PHY-1507', 'Thermodynamics', 'Average kinetic energy', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 7 (§13.4)', 'The absolute temperature of an ideal gas is reduced to half its value. The average translational kinetic energy of its molecules', '["becomes four times smaller", "becomes half", "stays the same", "becomes √2 times smaller"]', 1, '<K.E.> = (3/2)k_BT, so the average kinetic energy is directly proportional to T. Halving T halves the average kinetic energy.', '{"0": "A factor of four would need T to fall to a quarter.", "2": "Kinetic energy depends directly on temperature, so it must change.", "3": "√2 describes how the rms speed changes, not the kinetic energy."}'),
  ('PHY-1508', 'Thermodynamics', 'Average kinetic energy', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 7 (§13.4)', 'What is the total translational kinetic energy of the molecules in 1 mole of an ideal gas at 300 K? (R = 8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹)', '["about 2.5 kJ", "about 1.2 kJ", "about 3.7 kJ", "about 6.2 × 10⁻²¹ J"]', 2, 'Each molecule has (3/2)k_BT, and one mole has N_A molecules, so the total is (3/2)N_Ak_BT = (3/2)RT = 1.5 × 8.31 × 300 ≈ 3.7 × 10³ J.', '{"0": "2.5 kJ is RT; the factor 3/2 is missing.", "1": "1.2 kJ is ½RT, the share of one direction only.", "3": "6.2 × 10⁻²¹ J is the kinetic energy of a single molecule, not of a whole mole."}'),
  ('PHY-1509', 'Thermodynamics', 'Stellar evolution', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 10 (§13.6)', 'According to the book, what is true about red dwarf stars?', '["They explode as supernovae within a few million years.", "They are the most massive stars known.", "They have already completed their life cycles and become black holes.", "The universe is not yet old enough for any of them to have completed its life cycle."]', 3, 'Red dwarfs have low mass and use their fuel very efficiently, so they evolve very slowly. None has finished its life yet; models predict they will grow hotter and brighter and end as low-mass white dwarfs.', '{"0": "Supernovae end the lives of stars about ten times the Sun''s mass, not low-mass red dwarfs.", "1": "Red dwarfs are low-mass stars.", "2": "None has completed its life cycle, and they are expected to end as white dwarfs, not black holes."}'),
  ('PHY-1510', 'Thermodynamics', 'Stellar evolution', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 10 (§13.6)', 'When a low or intermediate mass star runs out of nuclear fuel, its outer layers are thrown off into space and form a', '["planetary nebula", "neutron star", "black hole", "protostar"]', 0, 'The core contracts into a white dwarf while the expelled outer layers spread into space as a planetary nebula.', '{"1": "Neutron stars form from the collapsed cores of much more massive stars.", "2": "Black holes come from the collapse of very massive stars.", "3": "A protostar is the early stage of a forming star, not the remains of a dying one."}'),
  ('PHY-1511', 'Thermodynamics', 'Stellar evolution', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 10 (§13.6)', 'In the red giant stage, stars with at least about half the mass of the Sun can fuse helium in their cores. This produces mainly', '["hydrogen and deuterium", "uranium and thorium", "iron and nickel only", "carbon and oxygen"]', 3, 'Helium fusion builds heavier nuclei such as carbon and oxygen. Only more massive stars continue fusing in further stages up to iron.', '{"0": "Hydrogen is the fuel of the earlier main-sequence stage; helium fusion does not make it.", "1": "Fusion in stars builds nuclei only up to iron, not heavy elements like uranium.", "2": "Iron is reached only at the end of successive fusion stages in very massive stars."}'),
  ('PHY-1512', 'Thermodynamics', 'Neutron stars', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 11 (§13.6 (Neutron stars))', 'Because of its extremely high density, the matter inside a neutron star is described in the book as behaving like a', '["normal ideal gas", "liquid of loosely bound atoms", "degenerate gas", "solid crystal of hydrogen"]', 2, 'Neutron stars are the densest objects after black holes; their matter behaves like a degenerate gas, and their strong gravity makes them hard to model.', '{"0": "An ideal gas has widely spaced particles; neutron-star matter is packed extremely densely.", "1": "Ordinary atoms cannot survive at neutron-star densities.", "3": "The book describes a degenerate gas, not a hydrogen crystal."}'),
  ('PHY-1513', 'Thermodynamics', 'Stellar evolution', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 12 (§13.6 (Example 13.4))', 'A star''s estimated gravitational pressure is about 1.7 × 10¹⁴ Pa, while its internal pressure is 1 × 10¹³ Pa. What does the book''s reasoning predict?', '["The star expands and may become a red giant.", "The star is in stable equilibrium.", "The star immediately becomes a white dwarf without contracting.", "The star contracts and may collapse to a neutron star or black hole."]', 3, 'Gravity (1.7 × 10¹⁴ Pa) is much stronger than the internal pressure (10¹³ Pa), so the balance fails inward: the star contracts and may eventually collapse to a neutron star or black hole.', '{"0": "Expansion happens when internal pressure is larger than the gravitational pressure, the opposite case.", "1": "Stability needs the two pressures to be nearly equal; here they differ by more than ten times.", "2": "A white dwarf is the end of a star that has used up its fuel; the book predicts contraction and possible collapse here."}'),
  ('PHY-1514', 'Thermodynamics', 'The Sun', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 11 (§13.6 (Our star))', 'According to the book, the Sun formed about 4.6 billion years ago and transfers its energy to Earth mainly by', '["conduction", "radiation", "convection through space", "sound waves"]', 1, 'Space between the Sun and Earth is almost empty, so heat cannot be carried by conduction or convection; the Sun''s energy reaches us mainly as radiation.', '{"0": "Conduction needs a material medium in contact, which space does not provide.", "2": "Convection needs a fluid to move; space is nearly empty.", "3": "Sound waves cannot travel through the vacuum of space."}'),
  ('PHY-1515', 'Thermodynamics', 'Kinetic theory and gas laws', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 4 (§13.3 (sidebar))', 'Using PV = ⅓Nm<v²>, why does the pressure of a fixed amount of gas rise when its volume is reduced at constant temperature?', '["At constant temperature <v²> is constant, so PV is constant and P rises as V falls.", "The molecules move faster when the volume is reduced.", "The mass of each molecule increases when compressed.", "The number of molecules increases when the volume is reduced."]', 0, 'At constant temperature the mean-square speed is fixed, and N and m do not change, so PV is constant (Boyle''s law). Squeezing the gas into a smaller volume makes the molecules hit the walls more often, so P rises.', '{"1": "At constant temperature the average speed does not change.", "2": "Compression does not change the mass of a molecule.", "3": "The amount of gas is fixed; only the space it fills changes."}'),
  ('PHY-1516', 'Thermodynamics', 'Statistical physics', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Thermal Physics, p. 8 (§13.5)', 'Statistical physics, used to describe systems with very large numbers of molecules, is the branch of physics that uses', '["probability theory", "only Newton''s laws applied to each molecule separately", "geometrical optics", "nuclear reactions"]', 0, 'With so many molecules it is impossible to follow each one, so statistical physics uses probability theory to describe how particles are spread over energy states.', '{"1": "Tracking every molecule separately is exactly what is impossible for ~10²³ particles.", "2": "Geometrical optics deals with light rays, not molecular energies.", "3": "Nuclear reactions are a different area of physics."}'),
  ('PHY-1517', 'Waves', 'Terms of oscillation', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 16 (§14.1)', 'In oscillatory motion, one vibration is', '["the motion from the mean position to one extreme position", "one complete round trip of the body about its mean position", "the maximum displacement from the mean position", "the number of oscillations made in one second"]', 1, 'A vibration is one complete cycle: from one extreme position to the other and back to the first, passing through the mean position. For a pendulum, A to B and back to A is one vibration.', '{"0": "Mean to one extreme is only a quarter of a vibration.", "2": "The maximum displacement is the amplitude.", "3": "Oscillations per second is the frequency."}'),
  ('PHY-1518', 'Waves', 'Terms of oscillation', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 17 (§14.1)', 'According to the book, the amplitude of a wave is a measure of', '["its speed", "its frequency", "the energy it carries", "its wavelength"]', 2, 'The greater the amplitude of a wave, the greater the energy it carries, and vice versa.', '{"0": "Wave speed depends on the medium, not on amplitude.", "1": "Frequency is the number of vibrations per second, independent of amplitude.", "3": "Wavelength is the length of one wave, not the size of the disturbance."}'),
  ('PHY-1519', 'Waves', 'Terms of oscillation', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 17 (§14.1)', 'Angular frequency of an oscillating body is defined as', '["angular displacement per unit time", "number of vibrations per unit time", "time taken for one vibration", "maximum displacement per unit time"]', 0, 'Angular frequency ω = θ/t, the angular displacement per unit time. For one cycle θ = 2π rad in time T, so ω = 2π/T = 2πf.', '{"1": "Vibrations per second is the frequency f, not ω.", "2": "The time for one vibration is the period T.", "3": "Maximum displacement per unit time is not a defined quantity of oscillation."}'),
  ('PHY-1520', 'Waves', 'Mass-spring system', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 19 (§14.3 (Example))', 'A particle in simple harmonic motion has an angular frequency of 4π rad s⁻¹. Its frequency and time period are', '["f = 4 Hz, T = 0.25 s", "f = 0.5 Hz, T = 2 s", "f = 12.6 Hz, T = 0.08 s", "f = 2 Hz, T = 0.5 s"]', 3, 'f = ω/2π = 4π ÷ 2π = 2 Hz, and T = 1/f = 0.5 s.', '{"0": "This takes f = ω/π instead of ω/2π.", "1": "This inverts the result: 0.5 Hz would need ω = π rad s⁻¹.", "2": "12.6 is ω itself in rad s⁻¹, not the frequency in hertz."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-1521', 'Waves', 'Mass-spring system', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 18 (§14.3)', 'A mass on a smooth horizontal surface is pulled to one side on a spring and released. Why does it move past the mean position instead of stopping there?', '["Because of its inertia, it keeps moving with the speed it has at the mean position.", "Because the restoring force is maximum at the mean position.", "Because friction pushes it forward.", "Because the spring constant becomes zero at the mean position."]', 0, 'At the mean position the restoring force is zero but the mass is moving at its greatest speed. Its inertia carries it past O until the spring, now compressed, slows it down and stops it at the other extreme.', '{"1": "The restoring force is zero at the mean position, since x = 0.", "2": "The surface is smooth, and friction would oppose motion, not drive it.", "3": "k is a property of the spring and does not change with position."}'),
  ('PHY-1522', 'Waves', 'Simple pendulum', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 20 (§14.3 (Simple pendulum))', 'For a simple pendulum displaced through an angle θ, why does the component mg cos θ not produce motion along the string?', '["It is always zero.", "It is equal to mg sin θ.", "It points towards the mean position.", "It is balanced by the tension in the string, which acts along the same line in the opposite direction."]', 3, 'There is no motion along the string, so the tension cancels mg cos θ. Only mg sin θ, acting along the arc, is left to pull the bob back to the mean position.', '{"0": "mg cos θ is zero only when θ = 90°, not for a normal swing.", "1": "The two components are equal only at θ = 45°.", "2": "mg sin θ, not mg cos θ, acts towards the mean position."}'),
  ('PHY-1523', 'Waves', 'Simple pendulum', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 22 (§14.3 (Example))', 'What is the length of a second pendulum (period 2 s) at a place where g = 9.8 m s⁻²?', '["about 0.25 m", "about 1.99 m", "about 0.99 m", "about 3.97 m"]', 2, 'From T = 2π√(ℓ/g), ℓ = gT²/4π² = 9.8 × 2² ÷ (4 × 3.14²) ≈ 0.99 m, about one metre.', '{"0": "0.25 m is the length for a period of 1 s.", "1": "1.99 m comes from dividing by 2π² instead of 4π².", "3": "3.97 m is the length for a period of 4 s."}'),
  ('PHY-1524', 'Waves', 'Simple pendulum', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 21 (§14.3 (Simple pendulum))', 'A pendulum clock keeps correct time in Karachi (sea level). It is taken to a hill station at high altitude, where g is slightly smaller. The clock will', '["gain time, because its period becomes shorter", "keep correct time, because the period does not depend on g", "stop, because a pendulum cannot swing at high altitude", "lose time, because its period becomes longer"]', 3, 'T = 2π√(ℓ/g). A smaller g makes T larger, so each swing takes longer and the clock runs slow (loses time).', '{"0": "A shorter period would need a larger g, not a smaller one.", "1": "The period depends on g as 1/√g.", "2": "A pendulum works anywhere gravity acts; only its period changes."}'),
  ('PHY-1525', 'Waves', 'Displacement, velocity, acceleration', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 25 (§14.4 (Example))', 'A 0.5 kg mass on a spring of spring constant 50 N m⁻¹ is performing SHM. What is its acceleration when its displacement is +0.03 m?', '["−3.0 m s⁻²", "+3.0 m s⁻²", "−0.3 m s⁻²", "−30 m s⁻²"]', 0, 'ω² = k/m = 50 ÷ 0.5 = 100 s⁻². a = −ω²x = −100 × 0.03 = −3.0 m s⁻², directed towards the mean position.', '{"1": "Acceleration in SHM is opposite to displacement, so it must be negative here.", "2": "This uses ω (10 rad s⁻¹) instead of ω².", "3": "This multiplies by k/m twice over; ω² = 100, not 1000."}'),
  ('PHY-1526', 'Waves', 'SHM and circular motion', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 24 (§14.4)', 'A point P moves uniformly round a circle and its projection Q moves along a diameter. The acceleration of Q is', '["zero at all times", "equal to the full centripetal acceleration of P at all times", "the component of P''s centripetal acceleration along the diameter", "directed away from the centre"]', 2, 'Q moves only along the diameter, so its acceleration is the component of P''s centripetal acceleration along that line: a = −x₀ω² cos θ = −ω²x.', '{"0": "Q speeds up and slows down, so it must have an acceleration.", "1": "Only the component along the diameter belongs to Q; it equals the full value only at the ends.", "3": "Centripetal acceleration and its component point towards the centre (the mean position)."}'),
  ('PHY-1527', 'Waves', 'Displacement, velocity, acceleration', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 27 (§14.5 (Example))', 'A body performs SHM according to x = x₀ cos ωt with x₀ = 0.10 m and ω = π rad s⁻¹. What is its displacement at t = 1/3 s?', '["0.087 m", "0.05 m", "0.10 m", "zero"]', 1, 'x = 0.10 cos(π × 1/3) = 0.10 cos 60° = 0.10 × 0.5 = 0.05 m.', '{"0": "0.087 m uses sin 60° instead of cos 60°.", "2": "0.10 m is the displacement at t = 0, the amplitude.", "3": "The body is at the mean position at t = 0.5 s (ωt = 90°), not at 1/3 s."}'),
  ('PHY-1528', 'Waves', 'Phase', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 26 (§14.5)', 'In x = x₀ cos(ωt + φ), the phase constant φ is fixed by', '["the mass of the oscillating body only", "the amplitude only", "the time period only", "the displacement and velocity of the body at t = 0"]', 3, 'φ tells where the body starts its cycle. Its value depends on the displacement and velocity of the particle at t = 0.', '{"0": "The mass affects ω, not the starting phase.", "1": "The amplitude is the size of the oscillation; it does not say where the motion starts.", "2": "The period sets ω, not the phase at t = 0."}'),
  ('PHY-1529', 'Waves', 'Phase', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 26 (§14.5)', 'Two oscillating systems are said to be oscillating in phase when the phase difference between them is', '["0° or 360°", "90°", "180°", "270°"]', 0, 'In-phase oscillators reach the same point of their cycles together, which means a phase difference of 0° (or a whole cycle, 360°).', '{"1": "90° means one is at an extreme when the other is at the mean position.", "2": "180° means they are out of phase, always moving opposite ways.", "3": "270° is a quarter cycle short of in phase, not in phase."}'),
  ('PHY-1530', 'Waves', 'Energy in SHM', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 29 (§14.7)', 'A spring of constant k is stretched from its mean position to x₀. The average force applied during the stretching, used to find the stored energy, is', '["kx₀", "kx₀/2", "2kx₀", "kx₀²/2"]', 1, 'The force grows uniformly from 0 at x = 0 to kx₀ at x = x₀, so its average is (0 + kx₀)/2 = kx₀/2. Work = average force × x₀ = ½kx₀².', '{"0": "kx₀ is the final (maximum) force, not the average.", "2": "Twice the maximum force is larger than any force actually applied.", "3": "½kx₀² is the work done (energy), not a force."}'),
  ('PHY-1531', 'Waves', 'Energy in SHM', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 30 (§14.7)', 'A body performs SHM with amplitude x₀. When its displacement is x₀/2, what fraction of its total energy is kinetic energy?', '["1/4", "1/2", "3/4", "1/√2"]', 2, 'P.E. = ½kx² = ½k(x₀/2)² = ¼ × ½kx₀², so P.E. is a quarter of the total. K.E. = total − P.E. = ¾ of the total.', '{"0": "1/4 is the potential energy fraction at x₀/2, not the kinetic energy.", "1": "K.E. and P.E. are equal at x = x₀/√2, not at x₀/2.", "3": "1/√2 is a displacement ratio, not an energy fraction."}'),
  ('PHY-1532', 'Waves', 'Free and forced oscillations', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 32 (§14.8)', 'In ideal free oscillations, with no resistive force acting,', '["the amplitude decreases steadily", "the frequency keeps increasing", "energy is fed in by an external periodic force", "the total energy and the amplitude stay constant"]', 3, 'A freely oscillating body vibrates at its natural frequency with nothing acting on it from outside. Without resistance no energy is lost, so the total energy and the amplitude stay constant.', '{"0": "A decreasing amplitude describes damped oscillations.", "1": "The natural frequency of a free oscillator is fixed.", "2": "An external periodic force produces forced oscillations, not free ones."}'),
  ('PHY-1533', 'Waves', 'Damping', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 32 (§14.9 (Interesting information))', 'A playground swing that slowly loses amplitude over many swings is the book''s example of', '["light damping", "critical damping", "heavy damping", "resonance"]', 0, 'Light damping removes energy gradually, so the amplitude shrinks slowly over many oscillations, as with a swing in a playground.', '{"1": "Critical damping returns the system to rest in the shortest time with no oscillation.", "2": "Heavy damping (like a pendulum in thick oil) stops the swinging and returns very slowly.", "3": "Resonance increases the amplitude; it does not reduce it."}'),
  ('PHY-1534', 'Waves', 'Resonance', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 35 (§14.10 (Microwave oven))', 'A microwave oven heats food mainly because the microwaves', '["heat the glass or plastic container, which then heats the food", "reflect off the food without being absorbed", "produce a nuclear reaction in the food", "make the water molecules in the food oscillate at the microwave frequency, and their collisions produce heat"]', 3, 'The microwave frequency matches the oscillation of water molecules, so they absorb energy and vibrate strongly; friction from their collisions turns this into heat. Containers without water are not heated.', '{"0": "Glass and plastic contain no water molecules, so they are not heated directly.", "1": "The food is heated because the waves are absorbed, not reflected.", "2": "Microwaves cause molecular oscillation, not nuclear reactions."}'),
  ('PHY-1535', 'Waves', 'Resonance', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 35 (§14.10 (Microwave oven))', 'According to the book, microwaves used in an oven have a wavelength of about', '["12 nm", "12 m", "12 km", "12 cm"]', 3, 'The book gives microwave-oven waves a frequency in the GHz range and a wavelength of about 12 cm. (A 2.45 GHz wave has λ = c/f ≈ 12 cm.)', '{"0": "12 nm is in the ultraviolet / X-ray range.", "1": "12 m is a radio wavelength (about 25 MHz).", "2": "12 km is a very low-frequency radio wavelength."}'),
  ('PHY-1536', 'Waves', 'Resonance', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 36 (§14.10 (Interesting information))', 'Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is mentioned in the book as an application of resonance because', '["X-rays are made to resonate inside the body", "sound waves cause the bones to vibrate", "radio-frequency radiation makes nuclei in the body oscillate and absorb energy", "the patient''s heart is made to beat at a resonant frequency"]', 2, 'In MRI, strong radio-frequency radiation makes nuclei oscillate and absorb energy; the pattern of absorbed energy is used to build a computer-enhanced image.', '{"0": "MRI uses radio waves, not X-rays.", "1": "MRI does not use sound waves to vibrate bones.", "3": "The heart rate is not involved in producing an MRI image."}'),
  ('PHY-1537', 'Waves', 'Resonance', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 36 (§14.10 (Interesting information))', 'A child on a swing can be pushed to a large amplitude with small pushes if the pushes are', '["given at the natural frequency of the swing", "as fast as possible", "given at random times", "given only once, very hard"]', 0, 'Pushes at the swing''s natural frequency are in step with its motion, so each one adds energy; this is resonance and the amplitude builds up.', '{"1": "Pushes much faster than the natural frequency would often oppose the motion.", "2": "Randomly timed pushes sometimes add and sometimes remove energy.", "3": "One push gives a single free oscillation that then dies away."}'),
  ('PHY-1538', 'Waves', 'Resonance', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 36 (§14.10)', 'Why should resonance be avoided in the design of aeroplane wings?', '["Resonance makes the wings lighter.", "If the frequency of forces such as turbulence or engine vibration matches the wings'' natural frequency, the large vibrations can be dangerous.", "Resonance stops the engine from working.", "Resonance reduces the lift on the wings to zero instantly."]', 1, 'Wings feel aerodynamic forces, turbulence and engine vibration. If any of these drives the wing at its natural frequency, resonance builds up a large amplitude that can damage the structure.', '{"0": "Resonance does not change the mass of the wing.", "2": "Engine operation is not the issue; the danger is large wing vibrations.", "3": "The danger is structural vibration, not an instant loss of lift."}'),
  ('PHY-1539', 'Waves', 'Standing waves', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Simple Harmonic Motion, p. 38 (§14.11 (Acoustic levitation))', 'In an acoustic levitator, a transducer and a reflector set up a standing ultrasonic wave. The small particles are trapped at the', '["antinodes, where the sound pressure is maximum", "transducer surface", "reflector surface", "nodes, where the sound pressure is minimum"]', 3, 'The incident and reflected waves form a standing wave. The pressure difference between antinodes (high) and nodes (low) pushes particles into the nodes, which act as acoustic traps.', '{"0": "The higher pressure at the antinodes pushes particles away towards the nodes.", "1": "The particles float in the air between the two ends, without contact.", "2": "The particles float without touching the reflector."}'),
  ('PHY-1540', 'Physical optics', 'Nature of light', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 42 (Introduction)', 'Who first proposed (in 1860) that light is an electromagnetic wave, produced by oscillating charges whose changing electric and magnetic fields regenerate each other?', '["Christiaan Huygens", "Isaac Newton", "James Clerk Maxwell", "Thomas Young"]', 2, 'Maxwell (1860) treated light as an electromagnetic wave: oscillating charges produce changing electric and magnetic fields that sustain each other and travel through vacuum.', '{"0": "Huygens proposed the wave theory in 1678, without the electromagnetic picture.", "1": "Newton proposed the corpuscular (particle) theory of light.", "3": "Young (1801) showed interference, proving light is a wave, but did not identify it as electromagnetic."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-1541', 'Physical optics', 'Wavefronts', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 43 (§15.1)', 'A point source in three-dimensional space produces wavefronts that are', '["plane", "spherical", "cylindrical", "straight lines"]', 1, 'Waves from a point source spread equally in all directions, so each wavefront is a sphere centred on the source. Far from the source, a small part of it looks plane.', '{"0": "Plane wavefronts come from a plane or line source, or from a very distant source.", "2": "Cylindrical wavefronts come from a long line source in 3D, not a point.", "3": "Straight-line wavefronts are the 2D picture of plane waves, such as from a vibrator bar in a ripple tank."}'),
  ('PHY-1542', 'Physical optics', 'Interference', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 45 (§15.3)', 'Which of these is NOT one of the conditions given in the book for two waves to produce an interference pattern?', '["The waves must be of the same type.", "The waves must be coherent.", "The waves must move in the same or nearly the same direction.", "The waves must have exactly equal amplitudes."]', 3, 'Interference needs waves of the same type, coherence (constant phase difference) and nearly the same direction. Different amplitudes still interfere; they only reduce the contrast between bright and dark regions.', '{"0": "Sound can only interfere with sound, light with light, so this is a condition.", "1": "A constant phase difference is essential for a steady pattern, so this is a condition.", "2": "Waves must travel in the same or almost the same direction, so this is a condition."}'),
  ('PHY-1543', 'Physical optics', 'Coherence', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 46 (§15.3 (Coherence))', 'If the two slits in an interference experiment were lit by two independent, non-coherent sources, what would an observer see on the screen?', '["A uniform band of light, because the pattern shifts too fast for the eye to follow", "A steady pattern of very sharp bright and dark fringes", "Complete darkness", "Only the central bright fringe"]', 0, 'Without a constant phase difference the fringe pattern changes continuously and far too quickly for the eye, so the screen looks evenly lit, with no definite bright and dark bands.', '{"1": "A steady fringe pattern needs coherent sources.", "2": "Light from both sources still reaches the screen, so it cannot be completely dark.", "3": "No stable fringe of any order forms without coherence."}'),
  ('PHY-1544', 'Physical optics', 'Coherence', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 45 (§15.3 (Coherence))', 'Two waves can keep a constant phase difference, and so act as coherent sources, only if', '["their amplitudes are equal", "they travel in opposite directions", "their frequencies are the same and stay constant", "one of them is a sound wave and the other is light"]', 2, 'A constant phase difference is possible only if both waves have the same, unchanging frequency; otherwise they drift in and out of phase.', '{"0": "Equal amplitudes improve contrast but are not needed for coherence.", "1": "Direction of travel is a separate condition for observing interference, not what makes sources coherent.", "3": "Different types of wave cannot interfere at all."}'),
  ('PHY-1545', 'Physical optics', 'Young''s double-slit experiment', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 48 (§15.5)', 'In Young''s experiment, a point C on the screen is the centre of the first bright fringe beside the central one. The path difference between the light from the two slits at C is', '["zero", "λ/2", "3λ/2", "λ"]', 3, 'At the central fringe the path difference is zero; at the first dark fringe it is λ/2; at the next bright fringe, C, one ray travels a whole wavelength further, so the path difference is λ and the waves arrive in phase.', '{"0": "Zero path difference gives the central bright fringe, not the next one.", "1": "λ/2 gives the first dark fringe.", "2": "3λ/2 gives the second dark fringe."}'),
  ('PHY-1546', 'Physical optics', 'Young''s double-slit experiment', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 47 (§15.4)', 'According to the book, why is a laser more convenient than a bright light bulb for the double-slit experiment?', '["A laser gives light of many different wavelengths.", "Light from a bulb cannot be diffracted.", "A laser does not need slits to show interference.", "A laser concentrates its light into a narrow, more intense beam."]', 3, 'The experiment works with a bright bulb too, but a laser is more convenient because its light is concentrated into a narrow, intense and coherent beam.', '{"0": "A laser gives nearly monochromatic light, a single wavelength.", "1": "Light from any source is diffracted by narrow slits.", "2": "The two slits are still needed to divide the laser light into two coherent sources."}'),
  ('PHY-1547', 'Physical optics', 'Fringe spacing', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 49 (§15.5)', 'In deriving the fringe positions in Young''s experiment, the book uses sin θ ≈ tan θ. This approximation is valid because', '["the slit separation is larger than the screen distance", "the light used is always red", "the fringe distances y on the screen are much smaller than the slit-to-screen distance L, so θ is small", "the slits are very wide"]', 2, 'The fringes lie close to the centre compared with L (about a metre), so θ is very small, and for small angles sin θ ≈ tan θ = y/L. This gives y = mλL/d.', '{"0": "The slit separation (a fraction of a millimetre) is far smaller than L.", "1": "The approximation does not depend on the colour of light.", "3": "Narrow, not wide, slits are used, and slit width is not the reason for the approximation."}'),
  ('PHY-1548', 'Physical optics', 'Thin films', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 50 (§15.6)', 'The complicated shapes of the coloured patterns seen on a soap bubble or an oil film on water are due to', '["variations in the thickness of the film", "the film absorbing all colours equally", "refraction of light only, with no interference", "the film emitting its own light"]', 0, 'Each colour interferes constructively where the path difference (set by the film''s thickness) suits its wavelength. Because the thickness varies from place to place, the colours form complex patterns.', '{"1": "Equal absorption of all colours would give no colours at all.", "2": "The colours come from interference between waves reflected at the two surfaces.", "3": "A soap film only reflects light falling on it."}'),
  ('PHY-1549', 'Physical optics', 'Newton''s rings', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 51 (§15.7)', 'Newton''s rings are best observed using monochromatic light. The book gives which source as an example?', '["A sodium lamp", "A filament (tungsten) bulb", "The Sun", "A candle flame"]', 0, 'A sodium lamp gives nearly monochromatic yellow light, so clear bright and dark rings are seen in the thin air film between the lens and the glass plate.', '{"1": "A filament bulb gives white light, which produces overlapping coloured rings.", "2": "Sunlight is white light, not monochromatic.", "3": "A candle flame gives a broad range of wavelengths."}'),
  ('PHY-1550', 'Physical optics', 'Diffraction grating', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 52 (§15.8)', 'According to the book, how can diffraction gratings be made?', '["By drilling holes with a laser in a mirror", "By melting glass into thin threads", "By scratching many equally spaced grooves on glass or metal with a diamond point", "By painting a thin film of oil on water"]', 2, 'Gratings are made by ruling many equally spaced grooves on a glass or metal surface with a diamond point, or by photographically reducing a pattern of black and white stripes.', '{"0": "The book does not describe gratings made by drilling holes in a mirror.", "1": "Glass threads are not a method of making gratings.", "3": "An oil film shows thin-film interference, not grating diffraction."}'),
  ('PHY-1551', 'Physical optics', 'Diffraction grating', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 53 (§15.8)', 'A typical grating for visible light has about 1000 slits per millimetre. Its grating spacing d is about', '["1000 mm", "1 nm", "1 mm", "1000 nm"]', 3, 'd is the reciprocal of the number of slits per unit length: d = 1 mm ÷ 1000 = 10⁻⁶ m = 1000 nm, the same order as visible wavelengths (400 to 700 nm).', '{"0": "This multiplies instead of taking the reciprocal.", "1": "1 nm is a thousand times too small.", "2": "1 mm is the length that contains all 1000 slits, not the spacing between them."}'),
  ('PHY-1552', 'Physical optics', 'Diffraction grating', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 52 (§15.8)', 'Why can a grating with thousands of slits measure wavelength much more precisely than a double slit?', '["Its maxima are much wider and fainter.", "Its principal maxima are in the same positions but much narrower and sharper.", "It produces no central maximum.", "It works only with X-rays."]', 1, 'Adding slits with the same spacing keeps the maxima at the same angles but makes them very narrow, so their angles, and hence the wavelength, can be measured very precisely.', '{"0": "The maxima become narrower and brighter, not wider and fainter.", "2": "A grating still produces a central (zero-order) maximum.", "3": "Ordinary gratings are used with visible light."}'),
  ('PHY-1553', 'Physical optics', 'X-ray diffraction', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Physical Optics, p. 53 (§15.9)', 'The study of crystal structure by X-ray diffraction was begun in 1914 by', '["Thomas Young and Augustin Fresnel", "Isaac Newton and Christiaan Huygens", "Joseph von Fraunhofer and James Maxwell", "W.H. Bragg and W.L. Bragg"]', 3, 'The father and son W.H. Bragg and W.L. Bragg began studying the atomic structure of crystals with X-rays in 1914 and found that X-rays reflect from crystal planes as if from a mirror.', '{"0": "Young and Fresnel worked on interference and diffraction of visible light.", "1": "Newton and Huygens proposed early theories of light in the 1600s.", "2": "Fraunhofer made the first grating and Maxwell described EM waves; neither began X-ray crystallography."}'),
  ('PHY-1554', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric potential', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 60 (§16.2)', 'Three point charges, +2 nC, +3 nC and −1 nC, are each 0.30 m from a point P. What is the electric potential at P? (1/4πε₀ = 9 × 10⁹ N m² C⁻²)', '["180 V", "120 V", "60 V", "zero"]', 1, 'Potential is a scalar, so the contributions add algebraically: V = (9 × 10⁹ × (2 + 3 − 1) × 10⁻⁹) ÷ 0.30 = 36 ÷ 0.30 = 120 V.', '{"0": "180 V adds all three charges as positive (6 nC), ignoring the sign of the −1 nC charge.", "2": "60 V uses only the +2 nC charge.", "3": "The charges do not cancel: their algebraic sum is +4 nC."}'),
  ('PHY-1555', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric potential', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 60 (§16.2)', 'The electric potential at a distance r from a point charge is 400 V. What is the potential at a distance 4r from the charge?', '["100 V", "25 V", "1600 V", "200 V"]', 0, 'V = q/(4πε₀r) is inversely proportional to r (not r²). Making r four times larger makes V one-quarter: 400 ÷ 4 = 100 V.', '{"1": "25 V uses 1/r², which is how the field, not the potential, varies.", "2": "Moving away from the charge lowers the potential; it does not raise it.", "3": "200 V would need the distance only to double."}'),
  ('PHY-1556', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric potential', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 61 (§16.2 (Example))', 'Charges of +9 μC and −3 μC are 40 cm apart. Apart from a point between them, the potential is also zero at a point on the line outside the charges. Where is it?', '["20 cm beyond the +9 μC charge", "10 cm beyond the −3 μC charge", "60 cm beyond the −3 μC charge", "20 cm beyond the −3 μC charge"]', 3, 'The zero point must be nearer the smaller charge, so it lies beyond the −3 μC charge. If it is y beyond that charge: 9/(40 + y) = 3/y, so 9y = 120 + 3y, giving y = 20 cm.', '{"0": "Near the larger +9 μC charge its potential always outweighs that of −3 μC, so V cannot be zero there.", "1": "10 cm gives 9/50 ≠ 3/10, so V is not zero there.", "2": "60 cm does not satisfy 9/(40 + y) = 3/y."}'),
  ('PHY-1557', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric potential energy', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 62 (§16.3)', 'A negative charge is released from rest in an electric field. It moves', '["from higher to lower potential", "along an equipotential surface", "it does not move at all", "from lower to higher potential"]', 3, 'A positive charge moves from higher to lower potential. A negative charge feels the opposite force, so it moves from lower to higher potential, losing potential energy and gaining kinetic energy.', '{"0": "That is how a positive charge moves.", "1": "No work is done along an equipotential, so a free charge does not stay on one.", "2": "A free charge in a field always feels a force and accelerates."}'),
  ('PHY-1558', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric potential energy', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 62 (§16.3)', 'An alpha particle (charge +2e) starts from rest and is accelerated through a potential difference of 1000 V. The kinetic energy it gains is', '["2000 eV", "1000 eV", "500 eV", "1.6 × 10⁻¹⁶ eV"]', 0, 'K.E. gained = qΔV = 2e × 1000 V = 2000 eV (= 3.2 × 10⁻¹⁶ J).', '{"1": "1000 eV is the energy of a particle with charge e, not 2e.", "2": "Charge 2e doubles the energy; it does not halve it.", "3": "1.6 × 10⁻¹⁶ is a value in joules for charge e, not an energy in eV."}'),
  ('PHY-1559', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric potential energy', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 63 (§16.3)', 'What is the electric potential energy of a +2 μC charge and a −3 μC charge placed 0.30 m apart? (1/4πε₀ = 9 × 10⁹ N m² C⁻²)', '["+0.18 J", "−0.18 J", "−0.60 J", "−1.8 J"]', 1, 'U = (1/4πε₀)(Qq/r) = 9 × 10⁹ × (2 × 10⁻⁶)(−3 × 10⁻⁶) ÷ 0.30 = −0.18 J. Unlike charges give a negative potential energy.', '{"0": "The product of a positive and a negative charge is negative.", "2": "−0.60 J divides by r² (0.09 m²); potential energy uses r, not r².", "3": "−1.8 J has the power of ten wrong."}'),
  ('PHY-1560', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric field and potential', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 65 (§16.5)', 'The book uses Gauss''s law for the electric field between the plates of a parallel plate capacitor carrying surface charge density σ in vacuum. This field is', '["σ/2ε₀", "σε₀", "σ/ε₀", "ε₀/σ"]', 2, 'Between closely spaced, oppositely charged parallel plates the field is uniform with magnitude E = σ/ε₀. With V = Ed and Q = σA this gives C = Aε₀/d.', '{"0": "σ/2ε₀ is the field of a single isolated sheet, not between two oppositely charged plates.", "1": "σε₀ has the wrong units for electric field.", "3": "This inverts the relation."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-1561', 'Electrostatics', 'Capacitance of a sphere', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 64 (§16.4)', 'An isolated metal sphere has a capacitance of 10 pF in air. If it is surrounded by a medium of dielectric constant 2.5, its capacitance becomes', '["4 pF", "10 pF", "12.5 pF", "25 pF"]', 3, 'C = 4πε₀ε_rR, so the capacitance is multiplied by ε_r: 10 pF × 2.5 = 25 pF.', '{"0": "A dielectric increases capacitance; it does not reduce it.", "1": "The surrounding medium does change the capacitance.", "2": "12.5 pF adds 2.5 instead of multiplying by it."}'),
  ('PHY-1562', 'Electrostatics', 'Parallel plate capacitor', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 65 (§16.5)', 'The SI unit of capacitance, the farad, is equal to', '["one coulomb per volt", "one volt per coulomb", "one joule per coulomb", "one coulomb per second"]', 0, 'C = Q/V, so 1 F = 1 C V⁻¹: a capacitor has 1 F if 1 C of charge gives a potential difference of 1 V. The unit is named after Michael Faraday.', '{"1": "This is the reciprocal of the farad.", "2": "A joule per coulomb is a volt.", "3": "A coulomb per second is an ampere."}'),
  ('PHY-1563', 'Electrostatics', 'Combination of capacitors', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 67 (§16.6 (Example))', 'A 60 μF capacitor is charged to 40 V and then connected in parallel to an uncharged capacitor. The common potential difference becomes 24 V. What is the capacitance of the second capacitor?', '["100 μF", "36 μF", "40 μF", "90 μF"]', 2, 'Charge is conserved. Q = 60 μF × 40 V = 2400 μC. Afterwards the 60 μF capacitor holds 60 × 24 = 1440 μC, so 960 μC moved to the second capacitor: C₂ = 960 μC ÷ 24 V = 40 μF.', '{"0": "100 μF is the total capacitance (2400 ÷ 24), not the second capacitor alone.", "1": "36 μF does not conserve charge: 60 × 24 + 36 × 24 = 2304 μC, not 2400 μC.", "3": "90 μF would bring the common voltage down to 16 V."}'),
  ('PHY-1564', 'Electrostatics', 'Combination of capacitors', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 68 (§16.6)', 'Two capacitors of unequal capacitance are connected in series across a battery. Which statement is correct?', '["The larger capacitor has the larger potential difference across it.", "Both have the same potential difference, equal to the battery voltage.", "The smaller capacitor has the larger potential difference across it.", "The larger capacitor stores the larger charge."]', 2, 'In series each capacitor carries the same charge Q, so V = Q/C. The smaller C therefore has the larger potential difference; the two voltages add up to the battery voltage.', '{"0": "With equal Q, V = Q/C is smaller for the larger capacitor.", "1": "Equal potential difference describes a parallel connection.", "3": "In series the charge on every capacitor is the same."}'),
  ('PHY-1565', 'Electrostatics', 'Combination of capacitors', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 68 (§16.6)', 'Capacitors of 2 μF, 3 μF and 6 μF are connected in series. Their equivalent capacitance is', '["11 μF", "1 μF", "3.7 μF", "6 μF"]', 1, '1/C = 1/2 + 1/3 + 1/6 = 3/6 + 2/6 + 1/6 = 1, so C = 1 μF. A series combination is always smaller than the smallest capacitor.', '{"0": "11 μF is the parallel combination.", "2": "3.7 μF is the plain average, which has no meaning here.", "3": "6 μF is the largest single value; a series combination is smaller than the smallest."}'),
  ('PHY-1566', 'Electrostatics', 'Energy in a capacitor', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 70 (§16.7)', 'The book gives which evidence that a charged capacitor stores energy?', '["Its plates become heavier.", "Its capacitance increases after charging.", "It glows in the dark.", "When its plates are joined by a thick wire, the wire becomes hot."]', 3, 'Joining the plates lets the charge flow and neutralise; the wire heats up. Heat is energy, so the charged capacitor must have stored that energy.', '{"0": "Charging moves electrons; it does not make the plates measurably heavier.", "1": "Capacitance depends on geometry and medium, not on whether the capacitor is charged.", "2": "A charged capacitor does not emit light."}'),
  ('PHY-1567', 'Electrostatics', 'Energy in a capacitor', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 71 (§16.7 (Example))', 'Capacitors of 3 μF and 6 μF are connected in series to a 90 V supply. What is the total energy stored in the combination?', '["8.1 × 10⁻³ J", "3.6 × 10⁻² J", "1.6 × 10⁻² J", "2.4 × 10⁻² J"]', 0, 'C_eq = (3 × 6)/(3 + 6) = 2 μF. Energy = ½C_eqV² = ½ × 2 × 10⁻⁶ × 90² = 8.1 × 10⁻³ J.', '{"1": "3.6 × 10⁻² J uses the parallel value, 9 μF.", "2": "1.6 × 10⁻² J leaves out the factor ½.", "3": "2.4 × 10⁻² J uses only the 6 μF capacitor across 90 V."}'),
  ('PHY-1568', 'Electrostatics', 'Charging and discharging', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 73 (§16.8 (Example))', 'The time constant of an RC circuit is RC. One ohm multiplied by one farad equals one', '["volt", "ampere", "coulomb", "second"]', 3, 'R = V/I = Vt/q and C = q/V, so RC = (Vt/q)(q/V) = t. Ω × F = s, which is why RC is called a time constant.', '{"0": "A volt is a joule per coulomb, not Ω × F.", "1": "An ampere is a coulomb per second.", "2": "A coulomb is a unit of charge."}'),
  ('PHY-1569', 'Electrostatics', 'Charging and discharging', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 76 (§16.9 (Example 16.10))', 'In an RC circuit, R = 2.0 kΩ and C = 50 μF. A capacitor charged to 20 V discharges through the resistor. What is the voltage across it after 0.10 s? (e ≈ 2.718)', '["about 2.7 V", "about 12.6 V", "about 10 V", "about 7.4 V"]', 3, 'τ = RC = 2000 × 50 × 10⁻⁶ = 0.10 s. After one time constant V = V₀e⁻¹ = 20 ÷ 2.718 ≈ 7.4 V.', '{"0": "2.7 V is V₀e⁻², the value after two time constants.", "1": "12.6 V is about 0.63 V₀, the fraction reached when charging, not what remains when discharging.", "2": "10 V is half of V₀, reached after 0.69RC (≈ 0.069 s)."}'),
  ('PHY-1570', 'Electrostatics', 'Charging and discharging', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 74 (§16.9)', 'For a capacitor discharging through a resistor, which graph shape correctly describes the charge against time?', '["A straight line falling at a constant rate to zero", "A curve that falls steeply at first and then flattens out", "A curve that rises steeply and then levels off", "A horizontal straight line"]', 1, 'Q = Q₀e^(−t/RC). The rate of fall depends on the charge left, so the graph is steep at the start and flattens as the charge gets smaller, like water draining from a tank.', '{"0": "A constant rate would need the current to stay the same, but the current falls as the charge falls.", "2": "A rising curve that levels off describes charging.", "3": "The charge does change; it decreases with time."}'),
  ('PHY-1571', 'Electrostatics', 'Charging and discharging', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 75 (§16.9)', 'When a capacitor discharges through a resistor, which quantities decay exponentially with the same time constant RC?', '["Only the charge", "Only the current", "The charge, the voltage and the current", "None of them; they fall linearly"]', 2, 'Q = Q₀e^(−t/RC), V = V₀e^(−t/RC) and I = I₀e^(−t/RC). All three fall by the same factor in the same time.', '{"0": "The voltage and current also decay, since V = Q/C and I = V/R.", "1": "The charge and voltage also decay with the same time constant.", "3": "The decay is exponential, not linear."}'),
  ('PHY-1572', 'Electrostatics', 'Charging and discharging', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 75 (§16.9)', 'Increasing the time constant RC of a discharging circuit makes the capacitor', '["discharge more slowly", "discharge more quickly", "discharge instantly", "never start discharging"]', 0, 'A larger RC means the circuit responds more slowly, so the charge takes longer to fall to 37% of its starting value.', '{"1": "A smaller time constant, not a larger one, gives a faster discharge.", "2": "Instant discharge would need RC = 0.", "3": "Discharge starts at once whatever the value of RC; it is just slower."}'),
  ('PHY-1573', 'Electrostatics', 'Uses of capacitors', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 77 (§16.10)', 'In an electric fan, the capacitor connected to the motor mainly', '["stores the fan''s speed setting", "provides extra power at start-up to help the motor overcome static friction and inertia", "converts AC into DC for the blades", "keeps the motor cool"]', 1, 'At switch-on the capacitor charges and gives the motor an extra boost of power, helping it overcome static friction and inertia so that it starts spinning quickly.', '{"0": "Speed settings are set by the regulator, not stored by the capacitor.", "2": "The fan motor runs on AC; this capacitor is not a rectifier.", "3": "Cooling is not the capacitor''s function."}'),
  ('PHY-1574', 'Electrostatics', 'Uses of capacitors', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 77 (§16.10)', 'Many refrigerators and air conditioners have a ''run capacitor'' as well as a ''start capacitor''. The run capacitor', '["works only during start-up and is then disconnected by a relay", "stores the cooling gas", "stays connected in the circuit to improve energy efficiency", "produces the flash for the interior light"]', 2, 'The start capacitor gives a ''jump start'' and is disconnected by a relay once the motor reaches speed. The run capacitor stays connected while the motor runs, improving energy efficiency.', '{"0": "That describes the start capacitor.", "1": "A capacitor stores charge and energy, not gas.", "3": "Flash tubes are used in cameras, not as fridge lights."}'),
  ('PHY-1575', 'Electrostatics', 'Uses of capacitors', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 77 (§16.10)', 'The flash tube of a camera flashgun contains which gas, ionised by the rapid discharge of a capacitor?', '["xenon", "neon", "hydrogen", "carbon dioxide"]', 0, 'When the shutter is pressed, the capacitor discharges rapidly into the flash tube. The large current ionises the xenon gas inside, giving a bright burst of light.', '{"1": "Neon is used in glowing signs, not in the flash tube described in the book.", "2": "The book names xenon, not hydrogen.", "3": "Carbon dioxide is not used in flash tubes."}'),
  ('PHY-1576', 'Electrostatics', 'Uses of capacitors', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Electrostatics / Static Electricity, p. 77 (§16.10)', 'In ECG and EEG machines and in imaging machines such as CT and MRI, the book says capacitors are used to', '["generate the X-rays", "store patient records", "produce the magnetic field directly", "filter out noise and unwanted signals"]', 3, 'Capacitors filter unwanted signals in ECG and EEG machines and reduce noise in imaging machines, improving image clarity.', '{"0": "X-rays come from an X-ray tube, not from a capacitor.", "1": "Records are stored digitally, not in capacitors.", "2": "The MRI magnetic field comes from large electromagnets."}'),
  ('PHY-1577', 'Alternating current', 'AC and its characteristics', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 82 (§17.1)', 'According to the book, a periodically varying current is called alternating only if each cycle has', '["two symmetrical half-cycles, one positive and one negative, and the same peak value in every cycle", "a positive half-cycle that is larger than the negative half-cycle", "a peak value that grows with each cycle", "no negative values at all"]', 0, 'For AC, each cycle has two exactly symmetrical halves, one positive and one negative, and the peak value stays the same in every cycle. The current reverses direction (or the voltage reverses polarity) every half-cycle.', '{"1": "Unequal halves would not be the symmetrical alternating waveform the book defines.", "2": "The peak value of AC stays constant from cycle to cycle.", "3": "A current with no negative values never reverses direction, so it is not alternating."}'),
  ('PHY-1578', 'Alternating current', 'AC and its characteristics', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 84 (§17.1 (sidebar))', 'The hissing or crackling sound often heard near high-voltage power lines is mainly caused by', '["the wires vibrating at the mains frequency", "birds sitting on the wires", "the air around the conductor being ionised by the strong electric field (corona discharge)", "rain falling on the insulators only"]', 2, 'When the electric field near the conductor exceeds the breakdown strength of air, the surrounding air ionises. This corona discharge can give a faint glow, radio noise and a hissing or crackling sound.', '{"0": "The book links the sound to ionisation of the air, not to the wires vibrating.", "1": "Birds are not the cause of the hissing.", "3": "Damp weather can make it worse, but the cause is ionisation of the air by the strong field."}'),
  ('PHY-1579', 'Alternating current', 'AC through a resistor', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 89 (§17.5 (Example))', 'A 1 kW heating element is connected to a 250 V (rms) AC supply. What are the rms current and the resistance of the element?', '["4 A and 62.5 Ω", "0.25 A and 1000 Ω", "4 A and 1000 Ω", "250 A and 1 Ω"]', 0, 'I = P/V = 1000 W ÷ 250 V = 4 A. R = V/I = 250 ÷ 4 = 62.5 Ω (or R = V²/P = 62 500 ÷ 1000).', '{"1": "0.25 A is V ÷ P, the inverted ratio, and 1000 Ω does not follow from it.", "2": "4 A is right, but the resistance is V/I = 62.5 Ω, not 1000 Ω.", "3": "250 A would need a power of 62.5 kW."}'),
  ('PHY-1580', 'Alternating current', 'Choke', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 92 (§17.7)', 'Why is a choke coil''s power factor almost zero?', '["Its inductance is almost zero.", "Its resistance is very small compared with its inductive reactance, so the current lags the voltage by nearly 90°.", "It is made of a semiconductor.", "It is connected only to DC supplies."]', 1, 'A choke has a large inductance (thick copper wire on a laminated soft-iron core) and very small resistance. The phase difference between current and voltage is nearly 90°, so cos θ ≈ 0 and almost no power is wasted as heat.', '{"0": "A choke has a very large inductance, increased by its iron core.", "2": "A choke is a copper coil on an iron core, not a semiconductor device.", "3": "For DC, X_L = 0 and a choke is just a low resistance; its use is in AC circuits."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-1581', 'Alternating current', 'Choke', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 92 (§17.7)', 'In a fluorescent tube light, the choke helps the lamp to start by', '["heating the tube to a high temperature", "storing light energy", "producing a transient high voltage that causes breakdown of the gas inside the tube", "converting AC to DC"]', 2, 'When the current through the choke changes suddenly, a large self-induced emf appears. This transient high voltage is enough to break down the gas in the tube and start the discharge.', '{"0": "The choke does not work by heating the tube.", "1": "A choke stores energy in its magnetic field, not as light.", "3": "A choke does not rectify; diodes do that."}'),
  ('PHY-1582', 'Alternating current', 'AC through a capacitor', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 93 (§17.8)', 'An AC ammeter in series with a capacitor shows a current, even though charge cannot cross the insulating gap between the plates. Why?', '["The dielectric becomes a conductor at high frequency.", "Charges leak continuously through the plates.", "The ammeter is reading the DC component only.", "The capacitor is charged first one way and then the other, so electrons flow to and fro in the connecting wires."]', 3, 'As the applied voltage reverses, the capacitor charges in one direction and then in the other. Electrons therefore surge back and forth in the wires connecting the plates, which is an alternating current.', '{"0": "The dielectric stays an insulator; no charge crosses it.", "1": "An ideal capacitor does not leak charge between its plates.", "2": "A capacitor blocks steady DC, so there is no DC component to read."}'),
  ('PHY-1583', 'Alternating current', 'AC and its characteristics', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 93 (§17.8 (So you know?))', 'Which scientist pioneered the generation, transmission and use of alternating current, which can be sent over much longer distances than direct current?', '["Michael Faraday", "Joseph Henry", "Thomas Edison", "Nikola Tesla"]', 3, 'Nikola Tesla (born 1856) pioneered AC generation and transmission and is often called the father of alternating current.', '{"0": "Faraday discovered electromagnetic induction, the basis of generators, but did not pioneer AC power systems.", "1": "Henry worked on self-induction; the henry is named after him.", "2": "Edison championed DC supply systems."}'),
  ('PHY-1584', 'Alternating current', 'Impedance', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 94 (§17.9 (Example))', 'An AC circuit has a peak voltage of 200 V and a peak current of 10 A. Its impedance is', '["20 Ω", "2000 Ω", "0.05 Ω", "14 Ω"]', 0, 'Z = V_rms/I_rms. Both rms values are peak ÷ √2, so the ratio of peak values gives the same result: Z = 200 ÷ 10 = 20 Ω.', '{"1": "This multiplies voltage by current, which gives a power-like quantity, not impedance.", "2": "This divides current by voltage.", "3": "14 Ω divides only the voltage by √2 and not the current."}'),
  ('PHY-1585', 'Alternating current', 'RC series circuit', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 96 (§17.10)', 'In an RC series circuit connected to an AC supply, where is electrical power dissipated?', '["Only in the capacitor", "In the capacitor and the resistor equally", "Nowhere; the average power is always zero", "Only in the resistor, since the capacitor stores energy and returns it"]', 3, 'The capacitor stores energy in its electric field and gives it back each cycle, so its average power is zero. The power used, P = VI cos θ = I²R, is dissipated in the resistor.', '{"0": "A capacitor does not dissipate power on average.", "1": "The capacitor takes no net power, so the sharing is not equal.", "2": "The resistor does dissipate power, so the average is not zero."}'),
  ('PHY-1586', 'Alternating current', 'RLC series circuit', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 98 (§17.12)', 'At a frequency well below resonance, an RLC series circuit behaves mainly like', '["an RC series circuit, because X_C is much larger than X_L", "an RL series circuit, because X_L is much larger than X_C", "a pure resistor, because X_L = X_C", "an open circuit with zero impedance"]', 0, 'At low frequency X_C = 1/ωC is large and X_L = ωL is small, so the capacitive reactance dominates and the circuit acts like an RC series circuit (current leads voltage).', '{"1": "X_L dominates at high frequency, not low frequency.", "2": "X_L = X_C only at the resonant frequency.", "3": "An open circuit has infinite, not zero, impedance; and neither describes the circuit here."}'),
  ('PHY-1587', 'Alternating current', 'RLC series circuit', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 100 (§17.12 (Example))', 'In an RLC series circuit the rms voltages are V_R = 32 V, V_L = 40 V and V_C = 16 V. What is the phase angle between the source voltage and the current?', '["about 53°, current leading", "about 90°, voltage leading", "about 37°, voltage leading", "zero"]', 2, 'tan θ = (V_L − V_C)/V_R = (40 − 16) ÷ 32 = 0.75, so θ ≈ 37°. Since V_L > V_C the circuit is inductive and the voltage leads the current. (The source voltage is √(32² + 24²) = 40 V.)', '{"0": "53° is tan⁻¹(32/24), the complement; and an inductive circuit has the current lagging, not leading.", "1": "90° would need V_R = 0.", "3": "θ is zero only at resonance, when V_L = V_C."}'),
  ('PHY-1588', 'Alternating current', 'RLC series circuit', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 99 (§17.12)', 'In an RLC series circuit, increasing the resistance R', '["removes all damping", "increases the damping, so oscillations of the current decay faster", "changes the resonant frequency to 1/(2πRC)", "makes the circuit resonate at every frequency"]', 1, 'The resistor introduces damping, just as a shock absorber does for a car''s spring. A higher resistance means more damping and faster decay of the current oscillations.', '{"0": "Increasing R adds damping; it does not remove it.", "2": "The resonant frequency is 1/(2π√LC) and does not depend on R.", "3": "A circuit resonates only where X_L = X_C."}'),
  ('PHY-1589', 'Alternating current', 'RLC parallel circuit', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 101 (§17.13)', 'At resonance in a parallel RLC circuit, how do the currents compare?', '["The source current is maximum and the branch currents are zero.", "All currents are equal to each other.", "The branch currents I_L and I_C can each be larger than the small source current.", "The current flows only through the resistor and the inductor."]', 2, 'At parallel resonance the impedance is maximum and the source current is minimum, but large currents circulate between L and C. Each branch current can exceed the source current, so the circuit gives current magnification.', '{"0": "At parallel resonance the source current is a minimum, not a maximum.", "1": "The inductor and capacitor branches carry much larger currents than the source.", "3": "At resonance, large currents flow in both the inductor and the capacitor branches."}'),
  ('PHY-1590', 'Alternating current', 'RLC parallel circuit', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 102 (§17.13)', 'At resonance, the power factor of a parallel RLC circuit is', '["zero", "unity (1)", "0.5", "infinite"]', 1, 'At resonance X_L = X_C, so the circuit behaves as purely resistive: voltage and current are in phase, θ = 0 and cos θ = 1.', '{"0": "Zero power factor belongs to a purely reactive circuit with a 90° phase difference.", "2": "0.5 would mean a 60° phase difference.", "3": "A power factor (cos θ) cannot exceed 1."}'),
  ('PHY-1591', 'Electronics', 'Rectification', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 105 (§17.14)', 'Mobile phone chargers use a full-wave bridge rectifier because', '["phone batteries need alternating current", "it raises the mains voltage for fast charging", "the battery must be charged with direct current, but the wall outlet supplies AC", "it removes the need for any diodes"]', 2, 'Phone batteries store DC, while the mains supply is AC. The bridge rectifier converts the AC from the outlet into DC so the battery can be charged.', '{"0": "Phone batteries store and are charged with DC, not AC.", "1": "Rectifiers change AC to DC; stepping voltage up or down is a transformer''s job.", "3": "A bridge rectifier is made of four diodes."}'),
  ('PHY-1592', 'Electronics', 'Rectification', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 102 (§17.14)', 'A PN junction diode can be used for rectification because it', '["conducts equally in both directions", "increases the frequency of AC", "stores large amounts of charge", "conducts a large current when forward biased but almost none when reverse biased"]', 3, 'A diode lets current flow in one direction (forward bias) and blocks it in the other (reverse bias). This one-way property turns alternating current into unidirectional current.', '{"0": "A device that conducts equally both ways cannot rectify.", "1": "A diode does not change the frequency of the supply.", "2": "Storing charge is what a capacitor does, not the reason a diode rectifies."}'),
  ('PHY-1593', 'Alternating current', 'Self-induction', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 107 (§17.16 (Example))', 'A coil has an inductive reactance of 160 Ω at 50 Hz. Its self-inductance is about', '["3.2 H", "8000 H", "0.51 H", "0.05 H"]', 2, 'X_L = 2πfL, so L = X_L ÷ 2πf = 160 ÷ (2 × 3.14 × 50) ≈ 0.51 H.', '{"0": "3.2 H is 160 ÷ 50, which leaves out the 2π.", "1": "8000 H multiplies X_L by f instead of dividing.", "3": "0.05 H divides by 2πf twice over."}'),
  ('PHY-1594', 'Alternating current', 'Energy in an inductor', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 108 (§17.16)', 'The energy stored in an inductor can never be negative because', '["it is proportional to the square of the current, U = ½LI²", "inductance can be negative or positive", "the current in an inductor is always zero", "it is stored in an electric field"]', 0, 'U = ½LI². Since I² is never negative and L is positive, the stored energy is never negative, whichever way the current flows.', '{"1": "Self-inductance is a positive property of the coil.", "2": "A current must flow for energy to be stored at all.", "3": "An inductor stores energy in its magnetic field, not an electric field."}'),
  ('PHY-1595', 'Electromagnetic induction', 'Mutual induction', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Alternating Current, p. 110 (§17.17)', 'Two coils have a mutual inductance of one henry if', '["the primary and secondary coils are joined by a wire of resistance 1 Ω", "the two coils have the same number of turns", "a steady current of 1 A in the primary induces 1 V in the secondary", "a current changing at 1 A s⁻¹ in the primary induces an emf of 1 V in the secondary"]', 3, 'M = ε_s ÷ (ΔI_p/Δt). One henry means a rate of change of primary current of 1 A s⁻¹ induces 1 V in the secondary. The unit is named after Joseph Henry.', '{"0": "Mutual inductance has nothing to do with a resistance joining the coils.", "1": "Equal numbers of turns do not define the henry.", "2": "A steady current induces no emf at all."}'),
  ('PHY-1596', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Quantum theory of radiation', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 118 (§18.2 (Example))', 'What is the energy of a photon of wavelength 620 nm, in electronvolts? (h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, c = 3.0 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹, 1 eV = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J)', '["about 2.0 eV", "about 3.2 × 10⁻¹⁹ eV", "about 0.5 eV", "about 20 eV"]', 0, 'E = hc/λ = (6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.0 × 10⁸) ÷ (620 × 10⁻⁹) ≈ 3.2 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. Dividing by 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J per eV gives about 2.0 eV.', '{"1": "3.2 × 10⁻¹⁹ is the energy in joules; the unit has not been converted.", "2": "0.5 eV is the reciprocal of the correct answer.", "3": "20 eV is ten times too large; such photons would be far into the ultraviolet."}'),
  ('PHY-1597', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'The photon', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 115 (§18.1)', 'A laser of power 2.0 mW emits light of wavelength 663 nm. About how many photons does it emit per second? (h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, c = 3.0 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹)', '["3.0 × 10⁻¹⁹", "6.7 × 10¹²", "6.7 × 10¹⁵", "1.5 × 10¹⁸"]', 2, 'Each photon has E = hc/λ = (6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.0 × 10⁸) ÷ (663 × 10⁻⁹) = 3.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. Photons per second = power ÷ E = 2.0 × 10⁻³ ÷ 3.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ ≈ 6.7 × 10¹⁵.', '{"0": "3.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J is the energy of one photon, not a number of photons.", "1": "6.7 × 10¹² takes the power as 2.0 μW instead of 2.0 mW.", "3": "1.5 × 10¹⁸ would need a power of about 0.45 W."}'),
  ('PHY-1598', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Photoelectric effect', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 116 (§18.2)', 'In the photoelectric experiment, the current in the circuit stops as soon as the light is cut off. This shows that', '["the battery alone drives the current", "the current is caused by the incident light", "the cathode has run out of electrons", "the stopping potential has become zero"]', 1, 'With the tube evacuated and the electrodes separated, current flows only while light falls on the cathode. Cutting off the light stops the current, proving the light releases the electrons that carry it.', '{"0": "If the battery alone drove the current, it would continue without light.", "2": "The metal has an enormous supply of free electrons.", "3": "The stopping potential is not involved when the anode is positive."}'),
  ('PHY-1599', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Photoelectric effect', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 117 (§18.2)', 'In the photoelectric effect, electrons are emitted with no noticeable time delay, even in weak light above the threshold frequency. Quantum theory explains this because', '["electrons slowly build up energy from the wave over a long time", "a whole photon''s energy is absorbed by a single electron at once", "weak light has a higher frequency", "the metal is heated by the light first"]', 1, 'A photon gives all its energy hf to one electron. If hf exceeds the work function, that electron is ejected instantly, whatever the intensity. In the wave picture, weak light would need time to deliver enough energy.', '{"0": "This is the wave-theory prediction that experiment contradicts.", "2": "Intensity and frequency are independent; weak light can have any frequency.", "3": "Photoemission is not a heating effect."}'),
  ('PHY-1600', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Photoelectric effect', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 118 (§18.2 (Example))', 'Light of wavelength 500 nm (photon energy 2.48 eV) falls on a metal and the stopping potential is found to be 0.60 V. What is the work function of the metal?', '["3.08 eV", "0.60 eV", "2.48 eV", "1.88 eV"]', 3, 'K.E._max = eV₀ = 0.60 eV. From hf = φ + K.E._max, φ = 2.48 − 0.60 = 1.88 eV.', '{"0": "3.08 eV adds the two energies instead of subtracting.", "1": "0.60 eV is the maximum kinetic energy of the electrons, not the work function.", "2": "2.48 eV is the photon energy."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-1601', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Pair production and annihilation', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 121 (§18.3)', 'What is the minimum energy a γ-ray photon must have to create an electron–positron pair? (rest mass energy of an electron = 0.51 MeV)', '["0.51 MeV", "2.04 MeV", "0.255 MeV", "1.02 MeV"]', 3, 'The photon must provide the rest mass energy of both particles: 2 × 0.51 = 1.02 MeV. Any energy above this becomes their kinetic energy.', '{"0": "0.51 MeV creates only one particle''s mass, but a pair is produced.", "1": "2.04 MeV is twice the minimum; it would leave 1.02 MeV as kinetic energy.", "2": "This is half of one particle''s rest energy."}'),
  ('PHY-1602', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Wave nature of particles', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 121 (§18.4)', 'What is the de Broglie wavelength of a 50 g ball moving at 20 m s⁻¹? (h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s)', '["6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ m", "6.6 × 10⁻³⁷ m", "1.3 × 10⁻³² m", "3.3 × 10⁻³⁵ m"]', 0, 'p = mv = 0.050 × 20 = 1.0 kg m s⁻¹, so λ = h/p = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ m. This is far too small for any wave effect to be detected.', '{"1": "6.6 × 10⁻³⁷ m takes the mass as 50 kg instead of 50 g.", "2": "1.3 × 10⁻³² m divides h by the mass only.", "3": "3.3 × 10⁻³⁵ m divides h by the speed only."}'),
  ('PHY-1603', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Davisson–Germer experiment', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 123 (§18.4)', 'Besides electrons, the book says diffraction patterns have also been observed with', '["only photons", "only light waves and sound waves", "protons, neutrons, and hydrogen and helium atoms", "nothing else; only electrons show wave behaviour"]', 2, 'Diffraction has also been seen with protons, neutrons, hydrogen atoms and helium atoms, which is strong evidence that all particles have a wave nature.', '{"0": "Photons are not the only other example; material particles also diffract.", "1": "The book''s point is about material particles, not just waves.", "3": "Many particles besides electrons have shown diffraction."}'),
  ('PHY-1604', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Wave–particle duality', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 124 (§18.5)', 'According to the book, light behaves', '["as a wave when it interacts with matter and as particles while it travels", "as a wave while it travels from source to detector and as a stream of photons when it interacts with matter", "only as a wave in all situations", "only as particles in all situations"]', 1, 'Light travels like a wave (showing interference and diffraction) but exchanges energy with matter as photons (as in the photoelectric effect). Both aspects are needed, but one experiment shows only one of them.', '{"0": "This reverses the two roles.", "2": "The photoelectric and Compton effects need the particle picture.", "3": "Interference and diffraction need the wave picture."}'),
  ('PHY-1605', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Wave–particle duality', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 123 (§18.5)', 'In J. J. Thomson''s experiment to measure e/m of the electron, which nature of the electron had to be assumed?', '["Its particle nature", "Its wave nature", "Both natures at the same time", "Neither; e/m does not depend on the nature of the electron"]', 0, 'Measuring e/m treats the electron as a charged particle deflected by fields, so its particle nature is assumed. Electron diffraction (Davisson–Germer, G.P. Thomson) shows its wave nature instead.', '{"1": "The wave nature is shown by electron diffraction, not by the e/m experiment.", "2": "No single experiment reveals both natures at once.", "3": "The experiment works by treating the electron as a particle with charge and mass."}'),
  ('PHY-1606', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Electron microscope', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 124 (§18.6)', 'A photograph of the image formed by an electron microscope is called a', '["spectrogram", "radiograph", "micrograph", "hologram"]', 2, 'The final image of the specimen, magnified by the projector lens, is recorded on a film; this photograph is called a micrograph.', '{"0": "A spectrogram records a spectrum.", "1": "A radiograph is an X-ray image.", "3": "A hologram is a 3D interference record made with laser light."}'),
  ('PHY-1607', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Atomic spectra', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 125 (§18.7)', 'Line spectra are useful in identifying elements because', '["every element gives the same set of lines", "the vapour of each element gives its own characteristic pattern of spectral lines", "line spectra contain all wavelengths", "only hydrogen produces spectral lines"]', 1, 'Each element has its own energy levels, so its vapour emits its own pattern of lines (spectral series). Matching the pattern identifies the element.', '{"0": "If all elements gave the same lines, spectra could not tell them apart.", "2": "A spectrum containing all wavelengths is continuous, not a line spectrum.", "3": "All elements produce line spectra; hydrogen is just the simplest."}'),
  ('PHY-1608', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Atomic spectra', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 125 (§18.7)', 'Using 1/λ = R_H(1/2² − 1/n²) with R_H = 1.097 × 10⁷ m⁻¹, what is the wavelength of the first (longest) line of the Balmer series (n = 3)?', '["about 486 nm", "about 365 nm", "about 122 nm", "about 656 nm"]', 3, '1/λ = R_H(1/4 − 1/9) = R_H × 5/36 = 1.097 × 10⁷ × 0.1389 ≈ 1.524 × 10⁶ m⁻¹, so λ ≈ 656 nm (red line).', '{"0": "486 nm is the second Balmer line, from n = 4.", "1": "365 nm is the series limit (n → ∞).", "2": "122 nm is the first Lyman line (n = 2 to n = 1), in the ultraviolet."}'),
  ('PHY-1609', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Atomic spectra', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 126 (§18.7)', 'In the book''s energy level diagram, the hydrogen levels are E₁ = −21.8 × 10⁻¹⁹ J and E₂ = −5.45 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. What is the wavelength of the photon emitted in the transition from n = 2 to n = 1? (hc = 1.99 × 10⁻²⁵ J m)', '["about 122 nm, in the ultraviolet (Lyman series)", "about 656 nm, in the visible (Balmer series)", "about 91 nm", "about 1875 nm, in the infrared"]', 0, 'ΔE = E₂ − E₁ = 16.35 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. λ = hc/ΔE = 1.99 × 10⁻²⁵ ÷ 1.635 × 10⁻¹⁸ ≈ 1.22 × 10⁻⁷ m = 122 nm. Transitions ending on n = 1 belong to the Lyman series in the UV.', '{"1": "Balmer lines end on n = 2, not n = 1.", "2": "91 nm uses the whole of |E₁|, which is the ionisation energy (the series limit).", "3": "Infrared lines like 1875 nm come from transitions ending on n = 3 (Paschen series)."}'),
  ('PHY-1610', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Atomic spectra', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 126 (§18.7)', 'White light passes through a cool gas of an element. How do the dark lines of this absorption spectrum compare with the bright lines of the same element''s emission spectrum?', '["They are at completely different wavelengths.", "The dark lines are at exactly the same wavelengths as bright lines of its emission spectrum.", "The absorption spectrum has no lines.", "The dark lines are always in the infrared."]', 1, 'Atoms absorb only photons whose energy matches a gap between their levels. These are the same energy gaps that give emitted photons, so the dark absorption lines line up with lines of the emission spectrum.', '{"0": "The same energy gaps cause both, so the wavelengths match.", "2": "The absorption spectrum shows dark lines on a continuous background.", "3": "The dark lines fall wherever the energy gaps place them, including the visible."}'),
  ('PHY-1611', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Atomic spectra', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 126 (§18.7)', 'In a hydrogen atom, the electron state with n = 1 is the ground state. The excited states (n = 2, 3, ...)', '["last for a very long time", "have very short lifetimes; the electron soon returns to a lower level and emits a photon", "cannot emit photons", "have lower energy than the ground state"]', 1, 'An atom absorbs energy in discrete amounts and is raised to an excited state. Excited states are short-lived; the electron quickly drops to a lower level, emitting photons that form the emission spectrum.', '{"0": "Excited states are very short-lived.", "2": "Photons are emitted precisely when the electron falls from an excited state.", "3": "The ground state is the lowest energy state."}'),
  ('PHY-1612', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Uncertainty principle', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 127 (§18.8)', 'To find the position of an electron more precisely, light of shorter wavelength must be used. What effect does this have?', '["It makes the electron''s momentum more uncertain, because each photon carries more momentum.", "It makes both position and momentum perfectly certain.", "It has no effect on the electron.", "It reduces the electron''s charge."]', 0, 'Δx ≈ λ, so shorter wavelength gives a smaller position uncertainty. But a photon''s momentum h/λ is then larger, so the collision disturbs the electron more and Δp grows. Δx Δp ≈ h.', '{"1": "Δx and Δp cannot both be made arbitrarily small.", "2": "A photon striking an electron changes its motion a lot.", "3": "The charge of an electron is fixed."}'),
  ('PHY-1613', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Uncertainty principle', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Quantum Physics, p. 127 (§18.8)', 'The book uses the uncertainty principle to argue that electrons cannot exist inside the nucleus. The reasoning is that confining an electron to about 10⁻¹⁴ m would', '["give it a negative mass", "give it zero energy", "give it an energy uncertainty of the order of GeV, which is not observed for electrons in atoms", "make it a proton"]', 2, 'Δx ≈ 10⁻¹⁴ m makes Δp ≈ h/Δx very large, corresponding to an energy of the order of GeV. No electrons with such energies are found in atoms, so electrons are not present inside the nucleus.', '{"0": "The uncertainty principle says nothing about negative mass.", "1": "Confinement raises the momentum and energy, it does not make them zero.", "3": "An electron cannot turn into a proton by being confined."}'),
  ('PHY-1614', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Nuclear reactor', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 139 (§19.2)', 'Cadmium is used for the control rods of a nuclear reactor because it', '["speeds up the neutrons", "absorbs a large number of neutrons without itself becoming unstable or radioactive", "releases extra neutrons to keep the reaction going", "slows the neutrons down so they cause more fission"]', 1, 'Cadmium can absorb many neutrons without becoming unstable. Inserting the rods removes neutrons from the core, reducing the number available for further fission and so controlling the chain reaction.', '{"0": "Control rods remove neutrons; they do not speed them up.", "2": "Releasing extra neutrons would make the reaction run faster, the opposite of control.", "3": "Slowing neutrons is the job of the moderator, not the control rods."}'),
  ('PHY-1615', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Nuclear reactor', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 140 (§19.3)', 'In an emergency, or for repairs, a reactor is shut down by', '["removing the moderator completely", "letting the control rods fall fully into the core so that the chain reaction stops", "increasing the amount of fuel", "pumping more coolant into the turbine"]', 1, 'Dropping the control rods all the way into the core absorbs so many neutrons that no further fission can be sustained, so the chain reaction stops.', '{"0": "The book describes shut-down by the control rods, not by removing the moderator.", "2": "More fuel would make the reaction harder, not easier, to stop.", "3": "Coolant in the turbine does not control the number of neutrons in the core."}'),
  ('PHY-1616', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Nuclear reactor', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 141 (§19.3)', 'According to the book, Pakistan''s nuclear power plants at Karachi and Chashma are all of which type?', '["Fast breeder reactors", "Fusion reactors", "Pressurized water reactors (PWR)", "Boiling water reactors without moderators"]', 2, 'The book states that the three plants at Karachi and four at Chashma, with a total output of more than 3000 MW, are all pressurized water reactors.', '{"0": "The book does not describe them as fast breeder reactors.", "1": "No fusion reactor yet produces electricity.", "3": "The book describes them as pressurized water reactors, which use a moderator."}'),
  ('PHY-1617', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Nuclear reactor', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 140 (§19.3)', 'At the Karachi Nuclear Power Plant (KANUPP), the steam coming out of the turbine is cooled using', '["heavy water", "liquid sodium", "sea water", "air only"]', 2, 'At KANUPP, heavy water is the moderator and carries heat from the core to the heat exchanger, while sea water cools the steam coming out of the turbine.', '{"0": "Heavy water is used as moderator and to carry heat from the core, not to cool the turbine steam.", "1": "Liquid sodium is used in some fast reactors, not at KANUPP.", "3": "The book names sea water as the cooling source."}'),
  ('PHY-1618', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Nuclear reactor', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 140 (§19.3)', 'Besides enriched uranium-235, which other nuclides does the book name as modern reactor fuels?', '["Plutonium-239 and uranium-233", "Iron-56 and carbon-12", "Cadmium-113 and boron-10", "Deuterium and tritium"]', 0, 'Early reactors used uranium enriched to about 2.4 to 3.0% U-235. Plutonium-239 and uranium-233 are also used as fuels now.', '{"1": "Iron-56 is the most stable nucleus and cannot be used for fission energy.", "2": "Cadmium and boron absorb neutrons; they are control rod materials.", "3": "Deuterium and tritium are fusion fuels."}'),
  ('PHY-1619', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Nuclear waste', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 141 (§19.3)', 'Nuclear waste is very hard to dispose of. The book gives the half-life of plutonium-239 in such waste as about', '["3.8 days", "1620 years", "24 000 years", "23.5 minutes"]', 2, 'Plutonium-239 has a half-life of about 24 000 years, so waste containing it stays dangerous for a very long time.', '{"0": "3.8 days is the half-life of radon gas.", "1": "1620 years is the half-life of radium-226.", "3": "23.5 minutes is the half-life of uranium-239."}'),
  ('PHY-1620', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Nuclear fusion', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 142 (§19.4)', 'A fusion reaction started by raising the fuel to an extremely high temperature, so that the nuclei move fast enough to overcome their repulsion, is called a', '["chain reaction", "thermonuclear reaction", "fission reaction", "annihilation reaction"]', 1, 'At millions of degrees the nuclei move fast enough to overcome electrostatic repulsion and fuse. Such temperature-driven fusion is called a thermonuclear reaction.', '{"0": "A chain reaction is the self-sustaining neutron process in fission.", "2": "Fission splits heavy nuclei rather than joining light ones.", "3": "Annihilation is a particle meeting its antiparticle."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-1621', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Nuclear fusion', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 142 (§19.4)', 'So far, uncontrolled fusion has been produced in the testing of the hydrogen bomb. How is a hydrogen bomb triggered?', '["By an atomic (fission) bomb, which produces the very high temperature needed", "By an electric spark", "By cooling the fuel to near absolute zero", "By a radioactive tracer"]', 0, 'Only for a very short time can the required temperature be reached, by exploding an atomic bomb. This triggers fusion in the hydrogen bomb.', '{"1": "A spark cannot provide millions of degrees.", "2": "Fusion needs extremely high, not low, temperatures.", "3": "Tracers are used for following processes, not for starting fusion."}'),
  ('PHY-1622', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Nuclear fusion', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 142 (§19.4 (Artificial Sun))', 'China''s EAST and HL-2M/HL-3 reactors are called ''artificial suns'' because they', '["shine with sunlight reflected from mirrors", "use fission of uranium like the Sun", "use magnetic fields to confine hydrogen isotopes heated to many millions of degrees in an attempt to fuse them", "orbit the Earth"]', 2, 'These experimental tokamaks confine super-heated hydrogen isotopes with strong magnetic fields, hotter than the Sun''s core, to mimic solar fusion as a source of clean energy.', '{"0": "They are fusion experiments, not mirror arrangements.", "1": "The Sun is powered by fusion, not fission.", "3": "They are ground-based reactors in China."}'),
  ('PHY-1623', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Radioactive decay', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 143 (§19.5)', 'The count rate recorded by a radiation detector placed near a source is', '["independent of the activity of the source", "directly proportional to the activity of the source", "always equal to zero after one half-life", "inversely proportional to the activity"]', 1, 'The count rate (counts per second recorded) is directly proportional to the activity, so it falls with time in the same way as the activity.', '{"0": "The count rate depends on how many nuclei decay per second.", "2": "After one half-life the count rate is halved, not zero.", "3": "More decays per second give more counts, not fewer."}'),
  ('PHY-1624', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Half-life', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 143 (§19.5)', 'Why can a radioactive sample never decay completely, according to the book?', '["Because the decay stops after a few half-lives", "Because in each half-life only half of the remaining nuclei decay, so an infinite time would be needed for all of them to decay", "Because new nuclei are created continuously", "Because the half-life increases with time"]', 1, 'After each half-life half of what remains is left, giving N₀/2, N₀/4, N₀/8, ... The number gets ever smaller but never reaches zero in a finite time.', '{"0": "Decay continues indefinitely; it does not stop.", "2": "A sample does not create new radioactive nuclei of the same kind.", "3": "The half-life of a nuclide is constant."}'),
  ('PHY-1625', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Radioactive decay', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 144 (§19.5)', 'The decay constant λ of a radioactive element is equal to', '["the time for half the nuclei to decay", "the number of nuclei present at the start", "the fraction of nuclei that decay per unit time", "the total energy released per decay"]', 2, 'From ΔN = −λNΔt, λ = (ΔN/N)/Δt, the fraction of the nuclei decaying per unit time. Its unit is s⁻¹. A larger λ means faster decay.', '{"0": "That is the half-life, which is related to λ by T½ = 0.693/λ.", "1": "N₀ is the initial number of nuclei, not the decay constant.", "3": "The decay constant is a rate, not an energy."}'),
  ('PHY-1626', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Half-life', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 145 (§19.5 (Example))', 'A radioactive isotope has a half-life of 8 days. A sample starts with 1600 undecayed atoms. How many atoms will have DECAYED after 24 days?', '["200", "800", "1200", "1400"]', 3, '24 days is 3 half-lives. The number left is 1600 × (½)³ = 200, so the number decayed is 1600 − 200 = 1400.', '{"0": "200 is the number left undecayed, not the number decayed.", "1": "800 is the number decayed after one half-life only.", "2": "1200 is the number decayed after two half-lives."}'),
  ('PHY-1627', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Half-life', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 144 (§19.5)', 'Which of these, as given in the book, has the longest half-life?', '["Radon gas", "Radium-226", "Uranium-238", "Uranium-239"]', 2, 'Uranium-238 has a half-life of about 4.5 × 10⁹ years, far longer than radium-226 (1620 years), radon (3.8 days) or uranium-239 (23.5 minutes).', '{"0": "Radon has a half-life of only 3.8 days.", "1": "Radium-226 has a half-life of 1620 years.", "3": "Uranium-239 has a half-life of 23.5 minutes."}'),
  ('PHY-1628', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Activity', 'hard', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 145 (§19.5 (Example))', 'Americium-241 has a molar mass of 241 g mol⁻¹. How many nuclei are there in a 2.41 μg sample? (N_A = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹)', '["6.02 × 10¹⁵", "6.02 × 10²¹", "1.45 × 10²⁰", "6.02 × 10¹⁸"]', 0, 'Moles = 2.41 × 10⁻⁶ g ÷ 241 g mol⁻¹ = 1.0 × 10⁻⁸ mol. N = 1.0 × 10⁻⁸ × 6.02 × 10²³ = 6.02 × 10¹⁵ nuclei.', '{"1": "6.02 × 10²¹ treats 2.41 μg as 2.41 g.", "2": "1.45 × 10²⁰ multiplies by the molar mass instead of dividing.", "3": "6.02 × 10¹⁸ treats 2.41 μg as 2.41 mg."}'),
  ('PHY-1629', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Radioactive tracers', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 146 (§19.6)', 'A radioactive isotope can be used as a tracer inside a living organism because', '["it behaves chemically just like the normal (stable) isotope, but its position can be found from the radiation it emits", "it is chemically very different from the stable isotope", "it never decays", "it has no effect on any detector"]', 0, 'Radioactive and stable isotopes of an element take part in the same chemical and biological processes. The ''tagged'' atoms can be followed with a radiation detector.', '{"1": "If it behaved differently, it would not show where the normal substance goes.", "2": "A tracer is useful precisely because it decays and emits detectable radiation.", "3": "Its radiation must be picked up by a detector for it to be traced."}'),
  ('PHY-1630', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Radioactive tracers', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 147 (§19.6)', 'According to the book, which organ selectively absorbs phosphorus, so that a phosphorus tracer collects there?', '["The liver", "The bones", "The thyroid gland", "The lungs"]', 1, 'Radio-iodine is absorbed mostly by the thyroid, phosphorus by the bones and cobalt by the liver, so each can serve as a tracer for that organ.', '{"0": "The book names cobalt for the liver.", "2": "The book names iodine for the thyroid gland.", "3": "The book does not give phosphorus for the lungs."}'),
  ('PHY-1631', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Medical uses of radiation', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 147 (§19.6)', 'Radiotherapy can destroy cancer cells more easily than normal cells because', '["cancer cells are not affected by radiation at all", "cancer cells multiply rapidly and absorb more radiation", "normal cells absorb all the radiation", "radiation only affects cells in the blood"]', 1, 'Experiments show that rapidly multiplying cancer cells absorb more radiation and are more easily destroyed by ionising radiation than normal cells.', '{"0": "If cancer cells were unaffected, radiotherapy would not work.", "2": "Normal cells are also damaged, which is why patients may feel ill, but less than cancer cells.", "3": "Radiation affects cells in any tissue it passes through."}'),
  ('PHY-1632', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Medical uses of radiation', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 147 (§19.6)', 'For treating skin cancer, the book names which radioisotopes, used with a carefully controlled dose?', '["Iodine-131 or sodium-24", "Uranium-235 or plutonium-239", "Carbon-12 or oxygen-16", "Phosphorus-32 or strontium-90"]', 3, 'Phosphorus-32 or strontium-90 may be used for skin cancer, with the dose controlled carefully to avoid harming healthy tissue.', '{"0": "Iodine-131 treats thyroid cancer and sodium-24 is used for blood circulation and plasma volume studies.", "1": "These are fission fuels, not therapy sources.", "2": "Carbon-12 and oxygen-16 are stable, not radioactive."}'),
  ('PHY-1633', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Biological effects of radiation', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 147 (§19.6)', 'Why do patients undergoing radiation treatment often feel ill?', '["The radiation also damages some of their healthy cells.", "The radiation makes them radioactive permanently.", "The radiation cools the body.", "The treatment removes all their red blood cells."]', 0, 'Ionising radiation does not affect only the cancer cells; it also damages some healthy cells, which can make the patient feel unwell.', '{"1": "External radiation therapy does not make the patient permanently radioactive.", "2": "Radiation does not work by cooling the body.", "3": "Treatment does not remove all red blood cells."}'),
  ('PHY-1634', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Medical uses of radiation', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 147 (§19.6)', 'In some cancer treatments, small radioactive sources called ''seeds'' are', '["swallowed to coat the stomach", "implanted in the malignant tissue for local, short-range treatment", "sprayed on the skin as a sunscreen", "placed in the patient''s food"]', 1, 'Seeds are implanted directly into the malignant tissue so that the short-range radiation acts locally on the tumour.', '{"0": "The seeds are implanted into the tumour, not swallowed.", "2": "They are not used as a skin coating.", "3": "They are placed in the tissue, not in food."}'),
  ('PHY-1635', 'Medical physics', 'Piezoelectric effect', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 153 (§20.1)', 'The word ''piezoelectric'' comes from the Greek word ''piezein'', which means', '["to shine", "to press", "to vibrate", "to slice"]', 1, '''Piezein'' means ''to press'': pressing (squeezing) a piezoelectric crystal produces an electric charge across it.', '{"0": "The word has nothing to do with shining.", "2": "Vibration is how a transducer is used, but it is not what the word means.", "3": "''To slice'' is the meaning of ''tomos'' in tomography."}'),
  ('PHY-1636', 'Medical physics', 'Radiation in medicine', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 153 (Introduction)', 'According to the book, gamma rays are used in nuclear medicine, such as PET scans and cancer treatment, mainly because of their', '["low frequency", "high penetrating power", "inability to ionise atoms", "long wavelength"]', 1, 'Gamma rays have very high energy and penetrating power, so they can pass out of the body to the detectors in PET and reach deep tumours in therapy.', '{"0": "Gamma rays have the highest frequencies in the EM spectrum.", "2": "Gamma rays are ionising radiation.", "3": "Gamma rays have the shortest wavelengths."}'),
  ('PHY-1637', 'Medical physics', 'Ultrasound imaging', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 154 (§20.2)', 'The acoustic impedance of a tissue, which decides how much ultrasound is reflected at a boundary, is determined by the tissue''s', '["colour and temperature", "electrical resistance", "density and the speed of sound in it", "atomic number only"]', 2, 'Acoustic impedance depends on the tissue density and the speed of sound in that tissue. The bigger the difference between two tissues, the stronger the echo from their boundary.', '{"0": "Colour and temperature do not set acoustic impedance.", "1": "Electrical resistance is unrelated to how sound is reflected.", "3": "Atomic number matters for X-ray absorption, not for ultrasound reflection."}'),
  ('PHY-1638', 'Medical physics', 'Ultrasound imaging', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 155 (§20.2)', 'In obstetrics, ultrasound is mainly used to', '["monitor fetal growth, estimate gestational age and identify abnormalities during pregnancy", "destroy kidney stones by heating", "image the bones of the skull in adults", "measure the radioactivity of a tracer"]', 0, 'In obstetrics, ultrasound monitors the growth of the fetus, estimates its gestational age and helps identify abnormalities, all without ionising radiation.', '{"1": "The book does not describe this use in obstetrics.", "2": "Obstetrics is concerned with pregnancy, not adult skull imaging.", "3": "Tracers are detected with gamma cameras or PET, not ultrasound."}'),
  ('PHY-1639', 'Medical physics', 'X-ray spectra', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 157 (§20.3)', 'The German word ''bremsstrahlung'', used for the continuous X-ray spectrum, means', '["braking radiation", "characteristic radiation", "nuclear radiation", "light radiation"]', 0, 'Bremsstrahlung means braking radiation. Electrons slowed down sharply by the target are strongly decelerated charges, and they emit X-rays.', '{"1": "Characteristic radiation is the line spectrum from inner-shell transitions.", "2": "Bremsstrahlung comes from decelerating electrons, not from the nucleus.", "3": "It is X-ray radiation, not visible light."}'),
  ('PHY-1640', 'Medical physics', 'CT scanning', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 159 (§20.4)', 'According to the book, in a CT machine', '["both the X-ray tube and the detectors stay fixed", "the X-ray tube rotates around the patient while the detectors stay in place", "the patient rotates while the tube stays fixed", "ultrasound is used instead of X-rays"]', 1, 'The X-ray tube rotates round the patient while the detectors stay in place, collecting information from all sides; the scanner then moves slightly along the body for the next slice.', '{"0": "Images from many different angles are needed, so the tube must move.", "2": "The book describes the tube, not the patient, rotating.", "3": "CT uses X-rays, combined with computer processing."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-1641', 'Medical physics', 'Radiotherapy', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Medical Physics, p. 161 (§20.5)', 'Radiotherapy in which the radiation is delivered from a machine placed outside the patient''s body is called', '["brachytherapy", "teletherapy", "tomography", "echocardiography"]', 1, 'Teletherapy delivers radiation externally from a machine. Brachytherapy places a radioactive source inside or close to the tumour.', '{"0": "Brachytherapy uses a source inside or close to the tumour.", "2": "Tomography is an imaging technique.", "3": "Echocardiography is ultrasound imaging of the heart."}'),
  ('PHY-1642', 'Space and environment', 'Earth''s climate system', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 174 (§21.9)', 'In the Earth''s climate system, the lithosphere means', '["the layer of air around the Earth", "all the living things on Earth", "the Earth''s upper rocky layer", "the oceans, lakes and rivers"]', 2, 'The five components are the atmosphere (air), hydrosphere (water), cryosphere (ice and permafrost), lithosphere (Earth''s upper rocky layer) and biosphere (living things).', '{"0": "The layer of air is the atmosphere.", "1": "Living things make up the biosphere.", "3": "Oceans, lakes and rivers belong to the hydrosphere."}'),
  ('PHY-1643', 'Space and environment', 'Earth''s climate system', 'easy', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 174 (§21.9)', 'Which component of the Earth''s climate system is made up of living things?', '["The cryosphere", "The biosphere", "The lithosphere", "The hydrosphere"]', 1, 'The biosphere is the living part of the climate system. It interacts with the other four components; a change in one part can cause changes in the others.', '{"0": "The cryosphere is ice and permafrost.", "2": "The lithosphere is the upper rocky layer.", "3": "The hydrosphere is the water."}'),
  ('PHY-1644', 'Space and environment', 'Blackbody radiation', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 168 (§21.4)', 'According to the book, the radiation emitted by a heated blackbody depends only on its', '["shape", "material", "temperature", "colour of paint"]', 2, 'Blackbody radiation depends only on temperature, not on the material or shape. As temperature rises, more energy is emitted and the peak shifts to shorter wavelengths.', '{"0": "The shape of the body does not change the blackbody spectrum.", "1": "The spectrum of an ideal blackbody does not depend on its material.", "3": "Surface colour matters for real bodies, but an ideal blackbody''s spectrum depends only on temperature."}'),
  ('PHY-1645', 'Space and environment', 'Satellite remote sensing', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Space and Environment, p. 174 (§21.8)', 'In satellite remote sensing, which step comes immediately after the radiation from the target is recorded by the sensor?', '["Source and illumination", "Interaction with the target", "Interpretation and analysis", "Transmission and reception of the data at a ground station"]', 3, 'The order is: source and illumination, interaction with the atmosphere, interaction with the target, recording by the sensor, transmission and reception, then interpretation and analysis.', '{"0": "Illumination by the source is the first step.", "1": "Interaction with the target happens before recording.", "2": "Interpretation comes after the data has been transmitted and processed."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

